planation helped the newcomer take part without feeling left behind. During the afternoon, the team checked how things were going. They did not assume that silence meant success. They asked people directly and made another small adjustment when it was needed.","By the end of the afternoon, Cedar Sports Club felt different. The original problem had become a chance to practise being welcoming. Noah returned a sketchbook to the table and wrote a note for the next group: “Check, listen, try, then improve.” The organiser thanked everyone, including the visitor whose question had changed the plan. Noah was pleased, but the best part was knowing how the group had reached its answer. They had not needed one person to know everything. They had needed people who were willing to think together."],"questions":[{"prompt":"What difficulty did the group face?","choices":["a delivery was delayed on the morning of an event","the planned activity left quieter visitors with no way to join","the first design kept falling over","two posters gave different opening times"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The organiser explains this difficulty on page 1.","hint":"Look back at what the group actually did."},{"prompt":"What did the group decide to do?","choices":["widen the base and test the design again","adapt the activity instead of cancelling it","invite people to choose how they wanted to take part","replace the old poster with a clear new notice"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The revised solution is described on page 3.","hint":"Look back at what the group actually did."},{"prompt":"Why did the group test a small version first?","choices":["To discover problems before using the full plan","To avoid listening to visitors","To make the task last forever","To hide the decision"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The trial let the group see what to improve before everyone arrived.","hint":"Think about what the trial revealed."}]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(id) DO UPDATE SET data=excluded.data;
INSERT INTO public.oh2_content(id,kind,data) VALUES('reader-8-3','book','{"title":"The Unexpected Delay at Cedar Sports Club","age":"13–15","genre":"Community fiction","college":false,"author":"ONE HOME original short-reader collection","pages":["Jules arrived at Cedar Sports Club before the afternoon activity began. The room was quiet, but there was plenty to prepare. On a small table lay a checklist, ready to be used. Then the organiser explained the difficulty: a delivery was delayed on the morning of an event. Jules had expected a simple afternoon and felt uncertain. Instead of pretending to know the answer, Jules asked for a few minutes to look carefully. There was still time to understand what had happened before anyone made a decision.","The first step was to check what supplies were already available. This took longer than making a quick guess, but it gave the group something useful to work with. Jules wrote down what was known and put a question mark beside what still needed checking. A friend suggested rushing ahead. “If we get the first part wrong, we may have to do everything twice,” Jules replied. Together they compared the information. One detail mattered more than the others: a plan had to work for the people who would actually use it.","Soon the group had a possible answer. They could adapt the activity instead of cancelling it. Jules explained the idea, then asked the others what might go wrong. At first, nobody spoke. After a moment, a younger visitor raised a concern. The group listened rather than dismissing it. They changed a small part of the plan and tried it on a limited scale. The trial was not perfect, but it showed them what to improve. Jules used a checklist to keep track of the next step.","When the visitors arrived, the group put the revised plan into practice. Someone who had missed the discussion asked why they had chosen this approach. Jules explained both the problem and the reason for the decision. That explanation helped the newcomer take part without feeling left behind. During the afternoon, the team checked how things were going. They did not assume that silence meant success. They asked people directly and made another small adjustment when it was needed.","By the end of the afternoon, Cedar Sports Club felt different. The original problem had become a chance to practise being resourceful. Jules returned a checklist to the table and wrote a note for the next group: “Check, listen, try, then improve.” The organiser thanked everyone, including the visitor whose question had changed the plan. Jules was pleased, but the best part was knowing how the group had reached its answer. They had not needed one person to know everything. They had needed people who were willing to think together."],"questions":[{"prompt":"What difficulty did the group face?","choices":["two posters gave different opening times","the first design kept falling over","a delivery was delayed on the morning of an event","two groups wanted to use the same space"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The organiser explains this difficulty on page 1.","hint":"Look back at what the group actually did."},{"prompt":"What did the group decide to do?","choices":["agree a timetable that gave each group a turn","widen the base and test the design again","replace the old poster with a clear new notice","adapt the activity instead of cancelling it"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The revised solution is described on page 3.","hint":"Look back at what the group actually did."},{"prompt":"Why did the group test a small version first?","choices":["To discover problems before using the full plan","To avoid listening to visitors","To make the task last forever","To hide the decision"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The trial let the group see what to improve before everyone arrived.","hint":"Think about what the trial revealed."}]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(id) DO UPDATE SET data=excluded.data;
