eeling left behind. During the afternoon, the team checked how things were going. They did not assume that silence meant success. They asked people directly and made another small adjustment when it was needed.","By the end of the afternoon, Lighthouse Reading Room felt different. The original problem had become a chance to practise being patient. Maya returned a ruler to the table and wrote a note for the next group: “Check, listen, try, then improve.” The organiser thanked everyone, including the visitor whose question had changed the plan. Maya was pleased, but the best part was knowing how the group had reached its answer. They had not needed one person to know everything. They had needed people who were willing to think together."],"questions":[{"prompt":"What difficulty did the group face?","choices":["new visitors found the entrance difficult to locate","two posters gave different opening times","two groups wanted to use the same space","the first design kept falling over"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The organiser explains this difficulty on page 1.","hint":"Look back at what the group actually did."},{"prompt":"What did the group decide to do?","choices":["widen the base and test the design again","replace the old poster with a clear new notice","agree a timetable that gave each group a turn","add simple signs at the confusing turning points"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The revised solution is described on page 3.","hint":"Look back at what the group actually did."},{"prompt":"Why did the group test a small version first?","choices":["To discover problems before using the full plan","To avoid listening to visitors","To make the task last forever","To hide the decision"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The trial let the group see what to improve before everyone arrived.","hint":"Think about what the trial revealed."}]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(id) DO UPDATE SET data=excluded.data;
INSERT INTO public.oh2_content(id,kind,data) VALUES('reader-15-5','book','{"title":"The Quiet Question at Lighthouse Reading Room","age":"15–16","genre":"Community fiction","college":false,"author":"ONE HOME original short-reader collection","pages":["Ellis arrived at Lighthouse Reading Room before the afternoon activity began. The room was quiet, but there was plenty to prepare. On a small table lay a pencil, ready to be used. Then the organiser explained the difficulty: two posters gave different opening times. Ellis had expected a simple afternoon and felt uncertain. Instead of pretending to know the answer, Ellis asked for a few minutes to look carefully. There was still time to understand what had happened before anyone made a decision.","The first step was to check the date and ask the organiser which notice was current. This took longer than making a quick guess, but it gave the group something useful to work with. Ellis wrote down what was known and put a question mark beside what still needed checking. A friend suggested rushing ahead. “If we get the first part wrong, we may have to do everything twice,” Ellis replied. Together they compared the information. One detail mattered more than the others: a plan had to work for the people who would actually use it.","Soon the group had a possible answer. They could replace the old poster with a clear new notice. Ellis explained the idea, then asked the others what might go wrong. At first, nobody spoke. After a moment, a younger visitor raised a concern. The group listened rather than dismissing it. They changed a small part of the plan and tried it on a limited scale. The trial was not perfect, but it showed them what to improve. Ellis used a pencil to keep track of the next step.","When the visitors arrived, the group put the revised plan into practice. Someone who had missed the discussion asked why they had chosen this approach. Ellis explained both the problem and the reason for the decision. That explanation helped the newcomer take part without feeling left behind. During the afternoon, the team checked how things were going. They did not assume that silence meant success. They asked people directly and made another small adjustment when it was needed.","By the end of the afternoon, Lighthouse Reading Room felt different. The original problem had become a chance to practise being thoughtful. Ellis returned a pencil to the table and wrote a note for the next group: “Check, listen, try, then improve.” The organiser thanked everyone, including the visitor whose question had changed the plan. Ellis was pleased, but the best part was knowing how the group had reached its answer. They had not needed one person to know everything. They had needed people who were willing to think together."],"questions":[{"prompt":"What difficulty did the group face?","choices":["two posters gave different opening times","two groups wanted to use the same space","new visitors found the entrance difficult to locate","a box of donated objects had lost its labels"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The organiser explains this difficulty on page 1.","hint":"Look back at what the group actually did."},{"prompt":"What did the group decide to do?","choices":["agree a timetable that gave each group a turn","replace the old poster with a clear new notice","add simple signs at the confusing turning points","write labels that explained where each object came from"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The revised solution is described on page 3.","hint":"Look back at what the group actually did."},{"prompt":"Why did the group test a small version first?","choices":["To discover problems before using the full plan","To avoid listening to visitors","To make the task last forever","To hide the decision"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The trial let the group see what to improve before everyone arrived.","hint":"Think about what the trial revealed."}]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(id) DO UPDATE SET data=excluded.data;
INSERT INTO public.oh2_content(id,kind,data) VALUES('reader-15-6','book','{"title":"The Shared Decision at Lighthouse Reading Room","age":"15–16","genre":"Community fiction","college":false,"author":"ONE HOME original short-reader collection","pages":["Asha arrived at Lighthouse Reading Room before the afternoon activity began. The room was quiet, but there was plenty to prepare. On a small table lay a folder, ready to be used. Then the organiser explained the difficulty: two groups wanted to use the same space. Asha had expected a simple afternoon and felt uncertain. Instead of pretending to know the answer, Asha asked for a few minutes to look carefully. There was still time to understand what had happened before anyone made a decision.","The first step was to listen to both groups and look at the booking sheet. This took longer than making a quick guess, but it gave the group something useful to work with. Asha wrote down what was known and put a question mark beside what still needed checking. A friend suggested rushing ahead. “If we get the first part wrong, we may have to do everything twice,” Asha replied. Together they compared the information. One detail mattered more than the others: a plan had to work for the people who would actually use it.","Soon the group had a possible answer. They could agree a timetable that gave each group a turn. Asha explained the idea, then asked the others what might go wrong. At first, nobody spoke. After a moment, a younger visitor raised a concern. The group listened rather than dismissing it. They changed a small part of the plan and tried it on a limited scale. The trial was not perfect, but it showed them what to improve. Asha used a folder to keep track of the next step.","When the visitors arrived, the group put the revised plan into practice. Someone who had missed the discussion asked why they had chosen this approach. Asha explained both the problem and the reason for the decision. That explanation helped the newcomer take part without feeling left behind. During the afternoon, the team checked how things were going. They did not assume that silence meant success. They asked people directly and made another small adjustment when it was needed.","By the end of the afternoon, Lighthouse Reading Room felt different. The original problem had become a chance to practise being fair. Asha returned a folder to the table and wrote a note for the next group: “Check, listen, try, then improve.” The organiser thanked everyone, including the visitor whose question had changed the plan. Asha was pleased, but the best part was knowing how the group had reached its answer. They had not needed one person to know everything. They had needed people who were willing to think together."],"questions":[{"prompt":"What difficulty did the group face?","choices":["new visitors found the entrance difficult to locate","two groups wanted to use the same space","a box of donated objects had lost its labels","the planned activity left quieter visitors with no way to join"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The organiser explains this difficulty on page 1.","hint":"Look back at what the group actually did."},{"prompt":"What did the group decide to do?","choices":["write labels that explained where each object came from","add simple signs at the confusing turning points","agree a timetable that gave each group a turn","invite people to choose how they wanted to take part"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The revised solution is described on page 3.","hint":"Look back at what the group actually did."},{"prompt":"Why did the group test a small version first?","choices":["To discover problems before using the full plan","To avoid listening to visitors","To make the task last forever","To hide the decision"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The trial let the group see what to improve before everyone arrived.","hint":"Think about what the trial revealed."}]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(id) DO UPDATE SET data=excluded.data;
INSERT INTO public.oh2_content(id,kind,data) VALUES('reader-15-7','book','{"title":"A Better Route at Lighthouse Reading Room","age":"15–16","genre":"Community fiction","college":false,"author":"ONE HOME original short-reader collection","pages":["Robin arrived at Lighthouse Reading Room before the afternoon activity began. The room was quiet, but there was plenty to prepare. On a small table lay a map, ready to be used. Then the organiser explained the difficulty: new visitors found the entrance difficult to locate. Robin had expected a simple afternoon and felt uncertain. Instead of pretending to know the answer, Robin asked for a few minutes to look carefully. There was still time to understand what had happened before anyone made a decision.","The first step was to walk the route with a first-time visitor. This took longer than making a quick guess, but it gave the group something useful to work with. Robin wrote down what was known and put a question mark beside what still needed checking. A friend suggested rushing ahead. “If we get the first part wrong, we may have to do everything twice,” Robin replied. Together they compared the information. One detail mattered more than the others: a plan had to work for the people who would actually use it.","Soon the group had a possible answer. They could add simple signs at the confusing turning points. Robin explained the idea, then asked the others what might go wrong. At first, nobody spoke. After a moment, a younger visitor raised a concern. The group listened rather than dismissing it. They changed a small part of the plan and tried it on a limited scale. The trial was not perfect, but it showed them what to improve. Robin used a map to keep track of the next step.","When the visitors arrived, the group put the revised plan into practice. Someone who had missed the discussion asked why they had chosen this approach. Robin explained both the problem and the reason for the decision. That explanation helped the newcomer take part without feeling left behind. During the afternoon, the team checked how things were going. They did not assume that silence meant success. They asked people directly and made another small adjustment when it was needed.","By the end of the afternoon, Lighthouse Reading Room felt different. The original problem had become a chance to practise being observant. Robin returned a map to the table and wrote a note for the next group: “Check, listen, try, then improve.” The organiser thanked everyone, including the visitor whose question had changed the plan. Robin was pleased, but the best part was knowing how the group had reached its answer. They had not needed one person to know everything. They had needed people who were willing to think together."],"questions":[{"prompt":"What difficulty did the group face?","choices":["the planned activity left quieter visitors with no way to join","a box of donated objects had lost its labels","new visitors found the entrance difficult to locate","a delivery was delayed on the morning of an event"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The organiser explains this difficulty on page 1.","hint":"Look back at what the group actually did."},{"prompt":"What did the group decide to do?","choices":["adapt the activity instead of cancelling it","write labels that explained where each object came from","invite people to choose how they wanted to take part","add simple signs at the confusing turning points"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The revised solution is described on page 3.","hint":"Look back at what the group actually did."},{"prompt":"Why did the group test a small version first?","choices":["To discover problems before using the full plan","To avoid listening to visitors","To make the task last forever","To hide the decision"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The trial let the group see what to improve before everyone arrived.","hint":"Think about what the trial revealed."}]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(id) DO UPDATE SET data=excluded.data;
INSERT INTO public.oh2_content(id,kind,data) VALUES('college-1','book','{"title":"Your first college visit","age":"15–16","genre":"College transition","college":true,"author":"ONE HOME original transition guide","pages":["1. Write down the course questions you want answered before you visit.\n\nTry this: make a short note about how this applies to your own plans. Think of one practical action you could take, who could help, and what information you still need. Your experience may differ from someone else’s, so use your college’s own guidance for dates and arrangements.\n\nBefore moving on, explain this step to yourself in your own words. You do not have to know everything at once. A useful next action is more helpful than a perfect plan that you never begin.","2. Ask to see the places where teaching and independent study happen.\n\nTry this: make a short note about how this applies to your own plans. Think of one practical action you could take, who could help, and what information you still need. Your experience may differ from someone else’s, so use your college’s own guidance for dates and arrangements.\n\nBefore moving on, explain this step to yourself in your own words. You do not have to know everything at once. A useful next action is more helpful than a perfect plan that you never begin.","3. Check who can explain entry requirements and application dates.\n\nTry this: make a short note about how this applies to your own plans. Think of one practical action you could take, who could help, and what information you still need. Your experience may differ from someone else’s, so use your college’s own guidance for dates and arrangements.\n\nBefore moving on, explain this step to yourself in your own words. You do not have to know everything at once. A useful next action is more helpful than a perfect plan that you never begin.","4. Compare your notes afterwards instead of relying only on first impressions.\n\nTry this: make a short note about how this applies to your own plans. Think of one practical action you could take, who could help, and what information you still need. Your experience may differ from someone else’s, so use your college’s own guidance for dates and arrangements.\n\nBefore moving on, explain this step to yourself in your own words. You do not have to know everything at once. A useful next action is more helpful than a perfect plan that you never begin.","5. The next step is to ask the college about any question still unanswered.\n\nTry this: make a short note about how this applies to your own plans. Think of one practical action you could take, who could help, and what information you still need. Your experience may differ from someone else’s, so use your college’s own guidance for dates and arrangements.\n\nBefore moving on, explain this step to yourself in your own words. You do not have to know everything at once. A useful next action is more helpful than a perfect plan that you never begin."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Which action belongs in step 1 of this guide?","choices":["Write down the course questions you want answered before you visit.","Ignore the instructions from your college.","Assume every college has the same rules.","Leave every question unanswered."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Write down the course questions you want answered before you visit.","hint":"Look back at the beginning of that step."},{"prompt":"Which action belongs in step 2 of this guide?","choices":["Ask to see the places where teaching and independent study happen.","Ignore the instructions from your college.","Assume every college has the same rules.","Leave every question unanswered."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Ask to see the places where teaching and independent study happen.","hint":"Look back at the beginning of that step."},{"prompt":"Which action belongs in step 3 of this guide?","choices":["Check who can explain entry requirements and application dates.","Ignore the instructions from your college.","Assume every college has the same rules.","Leave every question unanswered."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Check who can explain entry requirements and application dates.","hint":"Look back at the beginning of that step."}]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(id) DO UPDATE SET data=excluded.data;
INSERT INTO public.oh2_content(id,kind,data) VALUES('college-2','book','{"title":"Choosing a course","age":"15–16","genre":"College transition","college":true,"author":"ONE HOME original transition guide","pages":["1. Start with subjects and activities that interest you.\n\nTry this: make a short note about how this applies to your own plans. Think of one practical action you could take, who could help, and what information you still need. Your experience may differ from someone else’s, so use your college’s own guidance for dates and arrangements.\n\nBefore moving on, explain this step to yourself in your own words. You do not have to know everything at once. A useful next action is more helpful than a perfect plan that you never begin.","2. Read the actual course description, including how work is assessed.\n\nTry this: make a short note about how this applies to your own plans. Think of one practical action you could take, who could help, and what information you still need. Your experience may differ from someone else’s, so use your college’s own guidance for dates and arrangements.\n\nBefore moving on, explain this step to yourself in your own words. You do not have to know everything at once. A useful next action is more helpful than a perfect plan that you never begin.","3. Ask about typical assignments, practical sessions and independent work.\n\nTry this: make a short note about how this applies to your own plans. Think of one practical action you could take, who could help, and what information you still need. Your experience may differ from someone else’s, so use your college’s own guidance for dates and arrangements.\n\nBefore moving on, explain this step to yourself in your own words. You do not have to know everything at once. A useful next action is more helpful than a perfect plan that you never begin.","4. Compare entry requirements using the college’s current information.\n\nTry this: make a short note about how this applies to your own plans. Think of one practical action you could take, who could help, and what information you still need. Your experience may differ from someone else’s, so use your college’s own guidance for dates and arrangements.\n\nBefore moving on, explain this step to yourself in your own words. You do not have to know everything at once. A useful next action is more helpful than a perfect plan that you never begin.","5. A teacher or careers adviser can help you discuss your shortlist.\n\nTry this: make a short note about how this applies to your own plans. Think of one practical action you could take, who could help, and what information you still need. Your experience may differ from someone else’s, so use your college’s own guidance for dates and arrangements.\n\nBefore moving on, explain this step to yourself in your own words. You do not have to know everything at once. A useful next action is more helpful than a perfect plan that you never begin."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Which action belongs in step 1 of this guide?","choices":["Start with subjects and activities that interest you.","Ignore the instructions from your college.","Assume every college has the same rules.","Leave every question unanswered."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Start with subjects and activities that interest you.","hint":"Look back at the beginning of that step."},{"prompt":"Which action belongs in step 2 of this guide?","choices":["Read the actual course description, including how work is assessed.","Ignore the instructions from your college.","Assume every college has the same rules.","Leave every question unanswered."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Read the actual course description, including how work is assessed.","hint":"Look back at the beginning of that step."},{"prompt":"Which action belongs in step 3 of this guide?","choices":["Ask about typical assignments, practical sessions and independent work.","Ignore the instructions from your college.","Assume every college has the same rules.","Leave every question unanswered."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Ask about typical assignments, practical sessions and independent work.","hint":"Look back at the beginning of that step."}]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(id) DO UPDATE SET data=excluded.data;
INSERT INTO public.oh2_content(id,kind,data) VALUES('college-3','book','{"title":"Planning the journey","age":"15–16","genre":"College transition","college":true,"author":"ONE HOME original transition guide","pages":["1. Check the route from home to the correct campus.\n\nTry this: make a short note about how this applies to your own plans. Think of one practical action you could take, who could help, and what information you still need. Your experience may differ from someone else’s, so use your college’s own guidance for dates and arrangements.\n\nBefore moving on, explain this step to yourself in your own words. You do not have to know everything at once. A useful next action is more helpful than a perfect plan that you never begin.","2. Look at arrival times as well as the length of the journey.\n\nTry this: make a short note about how this applies to your own plans. Think of one practical action you could take, who could help, and what information you still need. Your experience may differ from someone else’s, so use your college’s own guidance for dates and arrangements.\n\nBefore moving on, explain this step to yourself in your own words. You do not have to know everything at once. A useful next action is more helpful than a perfect plan that you never begin.","3. Allow time to find a classroom when the campus is unfamiliar.\n\nTry this: make a short note about how this applies to your own plans. Think of one practical action you could take, who could help, and what information you still need. Your experience may differ from someone else’s, so use your college’s own guidance for dates and arrangements.\n\nBefore moving on, explain this step to yourself in your own words. You do not have to know everything at once. A useful next action is more helpful than a perfect plan that you never begin.","4. Ask the college about travel support and check current eligibility.\n\nTry this: make a short note about how this applies to your own plans. Think of one practical action you could take, who could help, and what information you still need. Your experience may differ from someone else’s, so use your college’s own guidance for dates and arrangements.\n\nBefore moving on, explain this step to yourself in your own words. You do not have to know everything at once. A useful next action is more helpful than a perfect plan that you never begin.","5. Keep a second travel plan for unexpected disruption.\n\nTry this: make a short note about how this applies to your own plans. Think of one practical action you could take, who could help, and what information you still need. Your experience may differ from someone else’s, so use your college’s own guidance for dates and arrangements.\n\nBefore moving on, explain this step to yourself in your own words. You do not have to know everything at once. A useful next action is more helpful than a perfect plan that you never begin."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Which action belongs in step 1 of this guide?","choices":["Check the route from home to the correct campus.","Ignore the instructions from your college.","Assume every college has the same rules.","Leave every question unanswered."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Check the route from home to the correct campus.","hint":"Look back at the beginning of that step."},{"prompt":"Which action belongs in step 2 of this guide?","choices":["Look at arrival times as well as the length of the journey.","Ignore the instructions from your college.","Assume every college has the same rules.","Leave every question unanswered."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Look at arrival times as well as the length of the journey.","hint":"Look back at the beginning of that step."},{"prompt":"Which action belongs in step 3 of this guide?","choices":["Allow time to find a classroom when the campus is unfamiliar.","Ignore the instructions from your college.","Assume every college has the same rules.","Leave every question unanswered."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Allow time to find a classroom when the campus is unfamiliar.","hint":"Look back at the beginning of that step."}]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(id) DO UPDATE SET data=excluded.data;
INSERT INTO public.oh2_content(id,kind,data) VALUES('college-4','book','{"title":"A week that works","age":"15–16","genre":"College transition","college":true,"author":"ONE HOME original transition guide","pages":["1. Put lessons and other fixed commitments into a weekly planner.\n\nTry this: make a short note about how this applies to your own plans. Think of one practical action you could take, who could help, and what information you still need. Your experience may differ from someone else’s, so use your college’s own guidance for dates and arrangements.\n\nBefore moving on, explain this step to yourself in your own words. You do not have to know everything at once. A useful next action is more helpful than a perfect plan that you never begin.","2. Add short, realistic study periods rather than filling every spare hour.\n\nTry this: make a short note about how this applies to your own plans. Think of one practical action you could take, who could help, and what information you still need. Your experience may differ from someone else’s, so use your college’s own guidance for dates and arrangements.\n\nBefore moving on, explain this step to yourself in your own words. You do not have to know everything at once. A useful next action is more helpful than a perfect plan that you never begin.","3. Leave room for breaks, meals and travel.\n\nTry this: make a short note about how this applies to your own plans. Think of one practical action you could take, who could help, and what information you still need. Your experience may differ from someone else’s, so use your college’s own guidance for dates and arrangements.\n\nBefore moving on, explain this step to yourself in your own words. You do not have to know everything at once. A useful next action is more helpful than a perfect plan that you never begin.","4. Review the plan after a week to see what was realistic.\n\nTry this: make a short note about how this applies to your own plans. Think of one practical action you could take, who could help, and what information you still need. Your experience may differ from someone else’s, so use your college’s own guidance for dates and arrangements.\n\nBefore moving on, explain this step to yourself in your own words. You do not have to know everything at once. A useful next action is more helpful than a perfect plan that you never begin.","5. Change the plan when you learn more about your workload.\n\nTry this: make a short note about how this applies to your own plans. Think of one practical action you could take, who could help, and what information you still need. Your experience may differ from someone else’s, so use your college’s own guidance for dates and arrangements.\n\nBefore moving on, explain this step to yourself in your own words. You do not have to know everything at once. A useful next action is more helpful than a perfect plan that you never begin."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Which action belongs in step 1 of this guide?","choices":["Put lessons and other fixed commitments into a weekly planner.","Ignore the instructions from your college.","Assume every college has the same rules.","Leave every question unanswered."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Put lessons and other fixed commitments into a weekly planner.","hint":"Look back at the beginning of that step."},{"prompt":"Which action belongs in step 2 of this guide?","choices":["Add short, realistic study periods rather than filling every spare hour.","Ignore the instructions from your college.","Assume every college has the same rules.","Leave every question unanswered."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Add short, realistic study periods rather than filling every spare hour.","hint":"Look back at the beginning of that step."},{"prompt":"Which action belongs in step 3 of this guide?","choices":["Leave room for breaks, meals and travel.","Ignore the instructions from your college.","Assume every college has the same rules.","Leave every question unanswered."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Leave room for breaks, meals and travel.","hint":"Look back at the beginning of that step."}]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(id) DO UPDATE SET data=excluded.data;
INSERT INTO public.oh2_content(id,kind,data) VALUES('college-5','book','{"title":"Asking for support","age":"15–16","genre":"College transition","college":true,"author":"ONE HOME original transition guide","pages":["1. Find out how to contact your tutor and student support team.\n\nTry this: make a short note about how this applies to your own plans. Think of one practical action you could take, who could help, and what information you still need. Your experience may differ from someone else’s, so use your college’s own guidance for dates and arrangements.\n\nBefore moving on, explain this step to yourself in your own words. You do not have to know everything at once. A useful next action is more helpful than a perfect plan that you never begin.","2. Explain the difficulty as clearly as you can, using a specific example.\n\nTry this: make a short note about how this applies to your own plans. Think of one practical action you could take, who could help, and what information you still need. Your experience may differ from someone else’s, so use your college’s own guidance for dates and arrangements.\n\nBefore moving on, explain this step to yourself in your own words. You do not have to know everything at once. A useful next action is more helpful than a perfect plan that you never begin.","3. Ask what the next step will be and when to expect a reply.\n\nTry this: make a short note about how this applies to your own plans. Think of one practical action you could take, who could help, and what information you still need. Your experience may differ from someone else’s, so use your college’s own guidance for dates and arrangements.\n\nBefore moving on, explain this step to yourself in your own words. You do not have to know everything at once. A useful next action is more helpful than a perfect plan that you never begin.","4. Keep useful contact details somewhere you can find them.\n\nTry this: make a short note about how this applies to your own plans. Think of one practical action you could take, who could help, and what information you still need. Your experience may differ from someone else’s, so use your college’s own guidance for dates and arrangements.\n\nBefore moving on, explain this step to yourself in your own words. You do not have to know everything at once. A useful next action is more helpful than a perfect plan that you never begin.","5. You can ask again if the first approach has not solved the problem.\n\nTry this: make a short note about how this applies to your own plans. Think of one practical action you could take, who could help, and what information you still need. Your experience may differ from someone else’s, so use your college’s own guidance for dates and arrangements.\n\nBefore moving on, explain this step to yourself in your own words. You do not have to know everything at once. A useful next action is more helpful than a perfect plan that you never begin."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Which action belongs in step 1 of this guide?","choices":["Find out how to contact your tutor and student support team.","Ignore the instructions from your college.","Assume every college has the same rules.","Leave every question unanswered."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Find out how to contact your tutor and student support team.","hint":"Look back at the beginning of that step."},{"prompt":"Which action belongs in step 2 of this guide?","choices":["Explain the difficulty as clearly as you can, using a specific example.","Ignore the instructions from your college.","Assume every college has the same rules.","Leave every question unanswered."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Explain the difficulty as clearly as you can, using a specific example.","hint":"Look back at the beginning of that step."},{"prompt":"Which action belongs in step 3 of this guide?","choices":["Ask what the next step will be and when to expect a reply.","Ignore the instructions from your college.","Assume every college has the same rules.","Leave every question unanswered."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Ask what the next step will be and when to expect a reply.","hint":"Look back at the beginning of that step."}]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(id) DO UPDATE SET data=excluded.data;
INSERT INTO public.oh2_content(id,kind,data) VALUES('college-6','book','{"title":"Managing an assignment","age":"15–16","genre":"College transition","college":true,"author":"ONE HOME original transition guide","pages":["1. Read the task and identify exactly what you must produce.\n\nTry this: make a short note about how this applies to your own plans. Think of one practical action you could take, who could help, and what information you still need. Your experience may differ from someone else’s, so use your college’s own guidance for dates and arrangements.\n\nBefore moving on, explain this step to yourself in your own words. You do not have to know everything at once. A useful next action is more helpful than a perfect plan that you never begin.","2. Break a large deadline into smaller planning, drafting and checking stages.\n\nTry this: make a short note about how this applies to your own plans. Think of one practical action you could take, who could help, and what information you still need. Your experience may differ from someone else’s, so use your college’s own guidance for dates and arrangements.\n\nBefore moving on, explain this step to yourself in your own words. You do not have to know everything at once. A useful next action is more helpful than a perfect plan that you never begin.","3. Keep a record of sources as you work.\n\nTry this: make a short note about how this applies to your own plans. Think of one practical action you could take, who could help, and what information you still need. Your experience may differ from someone else’s, so use your college’s own guidance for dates and arrangements.\n\nBefore moving on, explain this step to yourself in your own words. You do not have to know everything at once. A useful next action is more helpful than a perfect plan that you never begin.","4. Ask your teacher when an instruction is unclear.\n\nTry this: make a short note about how this applies to your own plans. Think of one practical action you could take, who could help, and what information you still need. Your experience may differ from someone else’s, so use your college’s own guidance for dates and arrangements.\n\nBefore moving on, explain this step to yourself in your own words. You do not have to know everything at once. A useful next action is more helpful than a perfect plan that you never begin.","5. Leave time to check the final work against the task.\n\nTry this: make a short note about how this applies to your own plans. Think of one practical action you could take, who could help, and what information you still need. Your experience may differ from someone else’s, so use your college’s own guidance for dates and arrangements.\n\nBefore moving on, explain this step to yourself in your own words. You do not have to know everything at once. A useful next action is more helpful than a perfect plan that you never begin."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Which action belongs in step 1 of this guide?","choices":["Read the task and identify exactly what you must produce.","Ignore the instructions from your college.","Assume every college has the same rules.","Leave every question unanswered."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Read the task and identify exactly what you must produce.","hint":"Look back at the beginning of that step."},{"prompt":"Which action belongs in step 2 of this guide?","choices":["Break a large deadline into smaller planning, drafting and checking stages.","Ignore the instructions from your college.","Assume every college has the same rules.","Leave every question unanswered."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Break a large deadline into smaller planning, drafting and checking stages.","hint":"Look back at the beginning of that step."},{"prompt":"Which action belongs in step 3 of this guide?","choices":["Keep a record of sources as you work.","Ignore the instructions from your college.","Assume every college has the same rules.","Leave every question unanswered."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Keep a record of sources as you work.","hint":"Look back at the beginning of that step."}]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(id) DO UPDATE SET data=excluded.data;
INSERT INTO public.oh2_content(id,kind,data) VALUES('college-7','book','{"title":"Meeting new people","age":"15–16","genre":"College transition","college":true,"author":"ONE HOME original transition guide","pages":["1. You do not need to become close friends with everyone immediately.\n\nTry this: make a short note about how this applies to your own plans. Think of one practical action you could take, who could help, and what information you still need. Your experience may differ from someone else’s, so use your college’s own guidance for dates and arrangements.\n\nBefore moving on, explain this step to yourself in your own words. You do not have to know everything at once. A useful next action is more helpful than a perfect plan that you never begin.","2. A simple question about a shared class can start a conversation.\n\nTry this: make a short note about how this applies to your own plans. Think of one practical action you could take, who could help, and what information you still need. Your experience may differ from someone else’s, so use your college’s own guidance for dates and arrangements.\n\nBefore moving on, explain this step to yourself in your own words. You do not have to know everything at once. A useful next action is more helpful than a perfect plan that you never begin.","3. Listen as well as speaking, and respect people’s boundaries.\n\nTry this: make a short note about how this applies to your own plans. Think of one practical action you could take, who could help, and what information you still need. Your experience may differ from someone else’s, so use your college’s own guidance for dates and arrangements.\n\nBefore moving on, explain this step to yourself in your own words. You do not have to know everything at once. A useful next action is more helpful than a perfect plan that you never begin.","4. Joining an activity can make repeated conversations easier.\n\nTry this: make a short note about how this applies to your own plans. Think of one practical action you could take, who could help, and what information you still need. Your experience may differ from someone else’s, so use your college’s own guidance for dates and arrangements.\n\nBefore moving on, explain this step to yourself in your own words. You do not have to know everything at once. A useful next action is more helpful than a perfect plan that you never begin.","5. Ask staff for support if someone makes you feel unsafe or excluded.\n\nTry this: make a short note about how this applies to your own plans. Think of one practical action you could take, who could help, and what information you still need. Your experience may differ from someone else’s, so use your college’s own guidance for dates and arrangements.\n\nBefore moving on, explain this step to yourself in your own words. You do not have to know everything at once. A useful next action is more helpful than a perfect plan that you never begin."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Which action belongs in step 1 of this guide?","choices":["You do not need to become close friends with everyone immediately.","Ignore the instructions from your college.","Assume every college has the same rules.","Leave every question unanswered."],"correct":0,"explanation":"You do not need to become close friends with everyone immediately.","hint":"Look back at the beginning of that step."},{"prompt":"Which action belongs in step 2 of this guide?","choices":["A simple question about a shared class can start a conversation.","Ignore the instructions from your college.","Assume every college has the same rules.","Leave every question unanswered."],"correct":0,"explanation":"A simple question about a shared class can start a conversation.","hint":"Look back at the beginning of that step."},{"prompt":"Which action belongs in step 3 of this guide?","choices":["Listen as well as speaking, and respect people’s boundaries.","Ignore the instructions from your college.","Assume every college has the same rules.","Leave every question unanswered."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Listen as well as speaking, and respect people’s boundaries.","hint":"Look back at the beginning of that step."}]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(id) DO UPDATE SET data=excluded.data;
INSERT INTO public.oh2_content(id,kind,data) VALUES('college-8','book','{"title":"Making useful notes","age":"15–16","genre":"College transition","college":true,"author":"ONE HOME original transition guide","pages":["1. Write the main idea before recording every small detail.\n\nTry this: make a short note about how this applies to your own plans. Think of one practical action you could take, who could help, and what information you still need. Your experience may differ from someone else’s, so use your college’s own guidance for dates and arrangements.\n\nBefore moving on, explain this step to yourself in your own words. You do not have to know everything at once. A useful next action is more helpful than a perfect plan that you never begin.","2. Use your own words when this helps you understand.\n\nTry this: make a short note about how this applies to your own plans. Think of one practical action you could take, who could help, and what information you still need. Your experience may differ from someone else’s, so use your college’s own guidance for dates and arrangements.\n\nBefore moving on, explain this step to yourself in your own words. You do not have to know everything at once. A useful next action is more helpful than a perfect plan that you never begin.","3. Mark questions you want to return to later.\n\nTry this: make a short note about how this applies to your own plans. Think of one practical action you could take, who could help, and what information you still need. Your experience may differ from someone else’s, so use your college’s own guidance for dates and arrangements.\n\nBefore moving on, explain this step to yourself in your own words. You do not have to know everything at once. A useful next action is more helpful than a perfect plan that you never begin.","4. After class, briefly check the notes and fill obvious gaps.\n\nTry this: make a short note about how this applies to your own plans. Think of one practical action you could take, who could help, and what information you still need. Your experience may differ from someone else’s, so use your college’s own guidance for dates and arrangements.\n\nBefore moving on, explain this step to yourself in your own words. You do not have to know everything at once. A useful next action is more helpful than a perfect plan that you never begin.","5. Use headings so the notes are easier to find when revising.\n\nTry this: make a short note about how this applies to your own plans. Think of one practical action you could take, who could help, and what information you still need. Your experience may differ from someone else’s, so use your college’s own guidance for dates and arrangements.\n\nBefore moving on, explain this step to yourself in your own words. You do not have to know everything at once. A useful next action is more helpful than a perfect plan that you never begin."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Which action belongs in step 1 of this guide?","choices":["Write the main idea before recording every small detail.","Ignore the instructions from your college.","Assume every college has the same rules.","Leave every question unanswered."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Write the main idea before recording every small detail.","hint":"Look back at the beginning of that step."},{"prompt":"Which action belongs in step 2 of this guide?","choices":["Use your own words when this helps you understand.","Ignore the instructions from your college.","Assume every college has the same rules.","Leave every question unanswered."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Use your own words when this helps you understand.","hint":"Look back at the beginning of that step."},{"prompt":"Which action belongs in step 3 of this guide?","choices":["Mark questions you want to return to later.","Ignore the instructions from your college.","Assume every college has the same rules.","Leave every question unanswered."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Mark questions you want to return to later.","hint":"Look back at the beginning of that step."}]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(id) DO UPDATE SET data=excluded.data;
INSERT INTO public.oh2_content(id,kind,data) VALUES('college-9','book','{"title":"Preparing an application","age":"15–16","genre":"College transition","college":true,"author":"ONE HOME original transition guide","pages":["1. Use the current instructions from the college you are applying to.\n\nTry this: make a short note about how this applies to your own plans. Think of one practical action you could take, who could help, and what information you still need. Your experience may differ from someone else’s, so use your college’s own guidance for dates and arrangements.\n\nBefore moving on, explain this step to yourself in your own words. You do not have to know everything at once. A useful next action is more helpful than a perfect plan that you never begin.","2. Keep a checklist of the information and evidence requested.\n\nTry this: make a short note about how this applies to your own plans. Think of one practical action you could take, who could help, and what information you still need. Your experience may differ from someone else’s, so use your college’s own guidance for dates and arrangements.\n\nBefore moving on, explain this step to yourself in your own words. You do not have to know everything at once. A useful next action is more helpful than a perfect plan that you never begin.","3. Describe your interests with specific examples.\n\nTry this: make a short note about how this applies to your own plans. Think of one practical action you could take, who could help, and what information you still need. Your experience may differ from someone else’s, so use your college’s own guidance for dates and arrangements.\n\nBefore moving on, explain this step to yourself in your own words. You do not have to know everything at once. A useful next action is more helpful than a perfect plan that you never begin.","4. Check your contact details carefully before submitting.\n\nTry this: make a short note about how this applies to your own plans. Think of one practical action you could take, who could help, and what information you still need. Your experience may differ from someone else’s, so use your college’s own guidance for dates and arrangements.\n\nBefore moving on, explain this step to yourself in your own words. You do not have to know everything at once. A useful next action is more helpful than a perfect plan that you never begin.","5. Save confirmation and ask about anything you do not understand.\n\nTry this: make a short note about how this applies to your own plans. Think of one practical action you could take, who could help, and what information you still need. Your experience may differ from someone else’s, so use your college’s own guidance for dates and arrangements.\n\nBefore moving on, explain this step to yourself in your own words. You do not have to know everything at once. A useful next action is more helpful than a perfect plan that you never begin."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Which action belongs in step 1 of this guide?","choices":["Use the current instructions from the college you are applying to.","Ignore the instructions from your college.","Assume every college has the same rules.","Leave every question unanswered."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Use the current instructions from the college you are applying to.","hint":"Look back at the beginning of that step."},{"prompt":"Which action belongs in step 2 of this guide?","choices":["Keep a checklist of the information and evidence requested.","Ignore the instructions from your college.","Assume every college has the same rules.","Leave every question unanswered."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Keep a checklist of the information and evidence requested.","hint":"Look back at the beginning of that step."},{"prompt":"Which action belongs in step 3 of this guide?","choices":["Describe your interests with specific examples.","Ignore the instructions from your college.","Assume every college has the same rules.","Leave every question unanswered."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Describe your interests with specific examples.","hint":"Look back at the beginning of that step."}]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(id) DO UPDATE SET data=excluded.data;
INSERT INTO public.oh2_content(id,kind,data) VALUES('college-10','book','{"title":"Your first week","age":"15–16","genre":"College transition","college":true,"author":"ONE HOME original transition guide","pages":["1. Check your timetable and campus information before setting out.\n\nTry this: make a short note about how this applies to your own plans. Think of one practical action you could take, who could help, and what information you still need. Your experience may differ from someone else’s, so use your college’s own guidance for dates and arrangements.\n\nBefore moving on, explain this step to yourself in your own words. You do not have to know everything at once. A useful next action is more helpful than a perfect plan that you never begin.","2. Bring the equipment your college has asked you to bring.\n\nTry this: make a short note about how this applies to your own plans. Think of one practical action you could take, who could help, and what information you still need. Your experience may differ from someone else’s, so use your college’s own guidance for dates and arrangements.\n\nBefore moving on, explain this step to yourself in your own words. You do not have to know everything at once. A useful next action is more helpful than a perfect plan that you never begin.","3. Notice where help desks and student services are located.\n\nTry this: make a short note about how this applies to your own plans. Think of one practical action you could take, who could help, and what information you still need. Your experience may differ from someone else’s, so use your college’s own guidance for dates and arrangements.\n\nBefore moving on, explain this step to yourself in your own words. You do not have to know everything at once. A useful next action is more helpful than a perfect plan that you never begin.","4. Ask when you are unsure; new students are still learning the routines.\n\nTry this: make a short note about how this applies to your own plans. Think of one practical action you could take, who could help, and what information you still need. Your experience may differ from someone else’s, so use your college’s own guidance for dates and arrangements.\n\nBefore moving on, explain this step to yourself in your own words. You do not have to know everything at once. A useful next action is more helpful than a perfect plan that you never begin.","5. At the end of the week, decide one useful thing to prepare for next week.\n\nTry this: make a short note about how this applies to your own plans. Think of one practical action you could take, who could help, and what information you still need. Your experience may differ from someone else’s, so use your college’s own guidance for dates and arrangements.\n\nBefore moving on, explain this step to yourself in your own words. You do not have to know everything at once. A useful next action is more helpful than a perfect plan that you never begin."],"questions":[{"prompt":"Which action belongs in step 1 of this guide?","choices":["Check your timetable and campus information before setting out.","Ignore the instructions from your college.","Assume every college has the same rules.","Leave every question unanswered."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Check your timetable and campus information before setting out.","hint":"Look back at the beginning of that step."},{"prompt":"Which action belongs in step 2 of this guide?","choices":["Bring the equipment your college has asked you to bring.","Ignore the instructions from your college.","Assume every college has the same rules.","Leave every question unanswered."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Bring the equipment your college has asked you to bring.","hint":"Look back at the beginning of that step."},{"prompt":"Which action belongs in step 3 of this guide?","choices":["Notice where help desks and student services are located.","Ignore the instructions from your college.","Assume every college has the same rules.","Leave every question unanswered."],"correct":0,"explanation":"Notice where help desks and student services are located.","hint":"Look back at the beginning of that step."}]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(id) DO UPDATE SET data=excluded.data;
INSERT INTO public.oh2_content(id,kind,data) VALUES('reading-check','test','{"title":"Your first reading check","questions":[{"section":1,"passage":"Amira arrived at Willow Library before the afternoon activity began. The room was quiet, but there was plenty to prepare. On a small table lay a notebook, ready to be used. Then the organiser explained the difficulty: a box of donated objects had lost its labels. Amira had expected a simple afternoon and felt uncertain. Instead of pretending to know the answer, Amira asked for a few minutes to look carefully. There was still time to understand what had happened before anyone made a decision.\n\nThe first step was to ask the donors for their notes. This took longer than making a quick guess, but it gave the group something useful to work with. Amira wrote down what was known and put a question mark beside what still needed checking. A friend suggested rushing ahead. “If we get the first part wrong, we may have to do everything twice,” Amira replied. Together they compared the information. One detail mattered more than the others: a plan had to work for the people who would actually use it.\n\nSoon the group had a possible answer. They could write labels that explained where each object came from. Amira explained the idea, then asked the others what might go wrong. At first, nobody spoke. After a moment, a younger visitor raised a concern. The group listened rather than dismissing it. They changed a small part of the plan and tried it on a limited scale. The trial was not perfect, but it showed them what to improve. Amira used a notebook to keep track of the next step.\n\nWhen the visitors arrived, the group put the revised plan into practice. Someone who had missed the discussion asked why they had chosen this approach. Amira explained both the problem and the reason for the decision. That explanation helped the newcomer take part without feeling left behind. During the afternoon, the team checked how things were going. They did not assume that silence meant success. They asked people directly and made another small adjustment when it was needed.\n\nBy the end of the afternoon, Willow Library felt different. The original problem had become a chance to practise being careful. Amira returned a notebook to the table and wrote a note for the next group: “Check, listen, try, then improve.” The organiser thanked everyone, including the visitor whose question had changed the plan. Amira was pleased, but the best part was knowing how the group had reached its answer. They had not needed one person to know everything. They had needed people who were willing to think together.","title":"The Missing Label at Willow Library","prompt":"Who is the main character?","choices":["Amira","The unnamed driver","The mayor","A visitor from another town"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Use details in the passage to support your answer."},{"section":1,"passage":"Amira arrived at Willow Library before the afternoon activity began. The room was quiet, but there was plenty to prepare. On a small table lay a notebook, ready to be used. Then the organiser explained the difficulty: a box of donated objects had lost its labels. Amira had expected a simple afternoon and felt uncertain. Instead of pretending to know the answer, Amira asked for a few minutes to look carefully. There was still time to understand what had happened before anyone made a decision.\n\nThe first step was to ask the donors for their notes. This took longer than making a quick guess, but it gave the group something useful to work with. Amira wrote down what was known and put a question mark beside what still needed checking. A friend suggested rushing ahead. “If we get the first part wrong, we may have to do everything twice,” Amira replied. Together they compared the information. One detail mattered more than the others: a plan had to work for the people who would actually use it.\n\nSoon the group had a possible answer. They could write labels that explained where each object came from. Amira explained the idea, then asked the others what might go wrong. At first, nobody spoke. After a moment, a younger visitor raised a concern. The group listened rather than dismissing it. They changed a small part of the plan and tried it on a limited scale. The trial was not perfect, but it showed them what to improve. Amira used a notebook to keep track of the next step.\n\nWhen the visitors arrived, the group put the revised plan into practice. Someone who had missed the discussion asked why they had chosen this approach. Amira explained both the problem and the reason for the decision. That explanation helped the newcomer take part without feeling left behind. During the afternoon, the team checked how things were going. They did not assume that silence meant success. They asked people directly and made another small adjustment when it was needed.\n\nBy the end of the afternoon, Willow Library felt different. The original problem had become a chance to practise being careful. Amira returned a notebook to the table and wrote a note for the next group: “Check, listen, try, then improve.” The organiser thanked everyone, including the visitor whose question had changed the plan. Amira was pleased, but the best part was knowing how the group had reached its answer. They had not needed one person to know everything. They had needed people who were willing to think together.","title":"The Missing Label at Willow Library","prompt":"Where does the story take place?","choices":["A railway carriage","Willow Library","A mountain campsite","An airport"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Use details in the passage to support your answer."},{"section":1,"passage":"Amira arrived at Willow Library before the afternoon activity began. The room was quiet, but there was plenty to prepare. On a small table lay a notebook, ready to be used. Then the organiser explained the difficulty: a box of donated objects had lost its labels. Amira had exp