t gave the group something useful to work with. Amira wrote down what was known and put a question mark beside what still needed checking. A friend suggested rushing ahead. “If we get the first part wrong, we may have to do everything twice,” Amira replied. Together they compared the information. One detail mattered more than the others: a plan had to work for the people who would actually use it.\n\nSoon the group had a possible answer. They could write labels that explained where each object came from. Amira explained the idea, then asked the others what might go wrong. At first, nobody spoke. After a moment, a younger visitor raised a concern. The group listened rather than dismissing it. They changed a small part of the plan and tried it on a limited scale. The trial was not perfect, but it showed them what to improve. Amira used a notebook to keep track of the next step.\n\nWhen the visitors arrived, the group put the revised plan into practice. Someone who had missed the discussion asked why they had chosen this approach. Amira explained both the problem and the reason for the decision. That explanation helped the newcomer take part without feeling left behind. During the afternoon, the team checked how things were going. They did not assume that silence meant success. They asked people directly and made another small adjustment when it was needed.\n\nBy the end of the afternoon, Willow Library felt different. The original problem had become a chance to practise being careful. Amira returned a notebook to the table and wrote a note for the next group: “Check, listen, try, then improve.” The organiser thanked everyone, including the visitor whose question had changed the plan. Amira was pleased, but the best part was knowing how the group had reached its answer. They had not needed one person to know everything. They had needed people who were willing to think together.","title":"The Missing Label at Willow Library","prompt":"Which object is mentioned?","choice