 why they had chosen this approach. Noah explained both the problem and the reason for the decision. That explanation helped the newcomer take part without feeling left behind. During the afternoon, the team checked how things were going. They did not assume that silence meant success. They asked people directly and made another small adjustment when it was needed.\n\nBy the end of the afternoon, Willow Library felt different. The original problem had become a chance to practise being welcoming. Noah returned a sketchbook to the table and wrote a note for the next group: “Check, listen, try, then improve.” The organiser thanked everyone, including the visitor whose question had changed the plan. Noah was pleased, but the best part was knowing how the group had reached its answer. They had not needed one person to know everything. They had needed people who were willing to think together.","title":"A Place for Everyone at Willow Library","prompt":"Who is the main character?","choices":["The unnamed driver","Noah","The mayor","A visitor from another town"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Use details in the passage to support your answer."},{"section":2,"passage":"Noah arrived at Willow Library before the afternoon activity began. The room was quiet, but there was plenty to prepare. On a small table lay a sketchbook, ready to be used. Then the organiser explained the difficulty: the planned activity left quieter visitors with no way to join. Noah had expected a simple afternoon and felt uncertain. Instead of pretending to know the answer, Noah asked for a few minutes to look carefully. There was still time to understand what had happened before anyone made a decision.\n\nThe first step was to offer a drawing table as well as a spoken discussion. This took longer than making a quick guess, but it gave the group something useful to work with. Noah wrote down what was known and put a question mark beside what still needed checking. A friend suggested rushing ahead. “If we get 