e it.\n\nSoon the group had a possible answer. They could adapt the activity instead of cancelling it. Jules explained the idea, then asked the others what might go wrong. At first, nobody spoke. After a moment, a younger visitor raised a concern. The group listened rather than dismissing it. They changed a small part of the plan and tried it on a limited scale. The trial was not perfect, but it showed them what to improve. Jules used a checklist to keep track of the next step.\n\nWhen the visitors arrived, the group put the revised plan into practice. Someone who had missed the discussion asked why they had chosen this approach. Jules explained both the problem and the reason for the decision. That explanation helped the newcomer take part without feeling left behind. During the afternoon, the team checked how things were going. They did not assume that silence meant success. They asked people directly and made another small adjustment when it was needed.\n\nBy the end of the afternoon, Willow Library felt different. The original problem had become a chance to practise being resourceful. Jules returned a checklist to the table and wrote a note for the next group: “Check, listen, try, then improve.” The organiser thanked everyone, including the visitor whose question had changed the plan. Jules was pleased, but the best part was knowing how the group had reached its answer. They had not needed one person to know everything. They had needed people who were willing to think together.","title":"The Unexpected Delay at Willow Library","prompt":"Who is the main character?","choices":["The mayor","The unnamed driver","Jules","A visitor from another town"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Use details in the passage to support your answer."},{"section":3,"passage":"Jules arrived at Willow Library before the afternoon activity began. The room was quiet, but there was plenty to prepare. On a small table lay a checklist, ready to be used. Then the organiser explained the difficulty: